/**
@mainpage Library

@brief @c libbotanik — greenhouse-control firmware for the Arduino OPTA PLC
       (STM32H747 dual-core ARM).

`libbotanik` is a C++11 library split into independent subsystems under
`src/libbotanik/`. It is platform-agnostic: every platform touch goes through a
Hardware Abstraction Layer, so the same code builds as a Linux simulator or for
the OPTA hardware. The application wires the subsystems together and runs them
from a single non-blocking loop — `src/botanik_sim/main.cpp` is the canonical
setup, and the OPTA `.ino` sketch does the equivalent.

This is the API reference. Browse the **Namespaces** and **Classes** tabs for the
full surface; the @ref testing page documents the unit suite, and the @ref manual
covers driving the system over its terminal.

@section main_subsystems Subsystems

| Subsystem    | Path         | Responsibility |
|--------------|--------------|----------------|
| **HAL**      | `hal/`       | Unified platform interface (clocks, memory ordering, stack/PLC introspection, serial). `simulator.h` and `opta.h` implement it. |
| **Scheduler**| `scheduler/` | Non-blocking fixed-period loop driver with overrun handling; delegates CPU-load, jitter, and stack-usage metrics to `PerfMonitor`. |
| **Terminal** | `terminal/`  | @ref Botanik::Terminal::AnsiTerminal drives a multiplexed page display; @ref Botanik::Terminal::InputParser decodes byte streams into events; @ref Botanik::Terminal::TextEditor is a byte-efficient line editor. |
| **Pages**    | `pages/`     | Full-screen views (`Shell`, `Logs`, `Home`, `Status`) behind a common @ref Botanik::Pages::Page interface. |
| **Logging**  | `logs/`      | @ref Botanik::Logs::Logger rate-limits with a dual token-bucket, then fans entries out to @ref Botanik::Logs::LogWriter "LogWriter"s. |
| **PLC I/O**  | `plcio/`     | @ref Botanik::PlcIO::PlcSyncHandler runs RX/TX state machines over shared memory with the PLC coprocessor; an emulator stands in for it on the simulator. |
| **Utils**    | `utils/`     | `BufferedSerial` (ring-buffered UART), `PerfMonitor` (loop CPU-load / jitter / stack high-water instrumentation), and time/uptime helpers. |

@section main_concepts Key concepts

- **Nothing blocks.** Every stateful object exposes a `step()` that does a small,
  bounded amount of work and returns immediately; the main loop calls them in a
  fixed order. Blocking would stall the RTOS and trip the watchdog on the OPTA.
- **HAL-only platform access.** Code never calls `<time.h>` or platform APIs
  directly — only `HAL::current_ms()`, `HAL::dsb()`, `HAL::ordered_copy()`, and
  friends. This is what makes the simulator possible.
- **PLC shared memory.** Host and PLC coprocessor communicate through shared
  memory using transactional read/write patterns and explicit ARM DSB barriers to
  avoid torn reads, with heartbeat / timeout / watchdog tracking.
- **Rate-limited logging.** A global bucket plus a per-topic bucket throttle log
  volume; suppressed-message counts are reported on a slower cadence.
- **Byte-efficient terminal.** The UI targets a low-bandwidth serial link, so the
  terminal/editor layer minimizes the escape sequences it emits.
- **Self-instrumenting loop.** `PerfMonitor` samples per-tick CPU load, scheduling
  jitter, and stack high-water usage (the last via stack painting), surfaced live
  in the status bar and the `stats` shell command.

@section main_architecture Architecture

- @subpage md_docs_2EVENT__DISPATCH — how input events flow from the hardware
  serial port to the active terminal page.
- @subpage md_docs_2PAGES — the page hierarchy and the screen contract every
  full-screen view shares.
- @subpage md_docs_2LOG__FLOW — how a log entry travels from a call site through
  rate limiting out to every sink.
*/
